{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[C#m]Gone are the days when the [E]ox fall down,
[B]You take up the yoke and plow the [A]fields around.
[C#m]Gone are the days when the [E]ladies said, "Please,
[A]Gentle Jack Jones won't you come to [E]me."
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[B]Brown-eyed women and red grenadine,
The [A]bottle was dusty but the [E]liquor was [B]clean.
[A]Sound of the thunder with the [E]rain pourin' [C#m]down,
And it [F#m]looks like the old man's [A]gettin' [E]on.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
1920 when he stepped to the bar,
Drank to the dregs of the whiskey jar.
1930 when the wall caved in,
He paid his way selling red-eyed gin.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
Delilah Jones was the mother of twins,
Two times over and the rest were sins.
Raised eight boys, only I turned bad,
Didn't get the lickin's that the other ones had.
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_chorus}
[Bm]Tumble down shack in [A]Big Foot [E]county.
[Bm]Snowed so hard that the [A]roof caved [E]in.
[C#m]Delilah [B]Jones went to [A]meet her [G#m]God,
And the [A]old man never was the same [E]again.
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
Daddy made whiskey and he made it well.
Cost two dollars and it burned like hell.
I cut hick'ry just to fire the still,
Drink down a bottle and be ready to kill.
{end_of_verse}

